================================================================================
1. OVERVIEW
================================================================================

PROJECT SUMMARY
---------------
This is a comprehensive, modular Neovim configuration optimized for Windows/MSYS2
with professional development capabilities. It uses lazy.nvim as the plugin
manager with 30+ plugins covering LSP, completion, git, snippets, and more.

ARCHITECTURE
------------
- Modular design with clear separation: config/, plugins/, keymaps/, snippets/
- Bootstrap sequence: settings → autocmds → utils → keymaps → tmp → lazy → pwsh
- Lazy.nvim auto-imports all plugin specs from lua/plugins/
- Uses Neovim 0.11+ native LSP API (not legacy nvim-lspconfig setup())
- 15 separate keymap files for organization
- Two-layer session management (custom + persisted.nvim)

CORE PLUGINS (30 Total)
------------------------
Infrastructure:
  - lazy.nvim (plugin manager)
  - plenary.nvim, mini.icons

LSP & Completion:
  - nvim-lspconfig (13 language servers)
  - mason.nvim, mason-lspconfig.nvim
  - blink.cmp (modern completion engine, faster than nvim-cmp)
  - LuaSnip (snippet engine)
  - conform.nvim (formatter manager)

Navigation:
  - telescope.nvim + fzf-native
  - oil.nvim (file browser)

Syntax:
  - nvim-treesitter (main branch)
  - nvim-ts-context-commentstring

Git:
  - gitsigns.nvim, diffview.nvim, neogit

UI:
  - tokyonight.nvim (transparent theme)
  - lualine.nvim (statusline)
  - fidget.nvim (LSP progress)
  - which-key.nvim (keymap hints)

Editing:
  - nvim-autopairs
  - Comment.nvim
  - surround.nvim
  - undotree.nvim
  - nvim-colorizer.lua
  - rainbow.nvim

Integration:
  - copilot.vim
  - tmux-navigator
  - persisted.nvim + local session manager

LANGUAGE SUPPORT (13 LSP Servers)
----------------------------------
- Lua (lua_ls)
- Python (pyright)
- JavaScript/TypeScript (ts_ls)
- HTML, CSS, JSON (vscode LSPs)
- Bash (bashls)
- Vim (vimls)
- Rust (rust_analyzer)
- PowerShell (powershell_es)
- AST Grep, Biome

KEYMAP ORGANIZATION
-------------------
Leader key: \ (backslash)
15 modular keymap files:
  - core.lua (346 lines) - basic editing, window nav
  - navi.lua - diagnostics, search navigation
  - space.lua - <space> leader mappings
  - sessions.lua - session management
  - help_search.lua - documentation navigation
  - telescope.lua - fuzzy finding
  - clipboard_diff.lua - clipboard operations
  - terminal.lua, quickfix.lua, lsp.lua, dir_navi.lua, etc.

CUSTOM FEATURES
---------------
- PowerShell integration with UTF-8 encoding fixes
- Dynamic LSP keymaps per-buffer
- Comprehensive snippet library (237 lines in all.lua)
- Session persistence per directory
- Configuration hot-reload (F9)
- Telescope multi-directory search
- Transparent background with manual highlight clearing


================================================================================
2. ISSUES / PROBLEMS
================================================================================

CRITICAL (Fix Immediately)
--------------------------
[C1✅] SYNTAX ERROR in lua/plugins/oil.lua:108
     Problem: vim.keymap.set() call outside of config table
     Location: Line 108
     Impact: May cause oil.nvim to fail loading
     Fix: Move the keymap.set() inside the config function

[C2] HARDCODED ABSOLUTE PATHS
     Locations:
       - lua/plugins/telescope.lua - "E:/Tools/BPY_Scripts/"
       - lua/plugins/telescope.lua - "C:/code_data/"
       - lua/plugins/personal-dev-env.lua - "C://dev//nvimdev//nvim-session-manager"
       - lua/config/pwsh.lua - Multiple Windows system paths
     Problem: Configuration won't work on other machines
     Impact: Breaks portability, telescope searches, session manager
     Fix: Use environment variables or create a local_paths.lua config

[C3] PRODUCTION CODE LOADING EXPERIMENTAL FEATURES
     Location: init.lua line requiring "tmp.init"
     Problem: tmp/ folder auto-loads in production
     Impact: Unstable/unfinished code may break config
     Fix: Comment out require("tmp.init") or move tmp/ outside nvim directory


HIGH PRIORITY
-------------
[H1✅] KEYMAP CONFLICTS - Multiple bindings for same key
     Conflicts found:
       - <C-l>: mapped in cmp.lua, navi.lua, telescope.lua, oil.lua
       - <C-j/k>: used for both snippet navigation and window movement
       - <Tab>: conflicts between blink.cmp and LuaSnip
     Impact: Unpredictable behavior, one mapping silently overrides others
     Fix: Audit all keymaps, document priorities, resolve conflicts
     Suggested: Create keymap_audit.lua tool to detect conflicts

[H2✅] NO ERROR HANDLING in PowerShell Setup
     Location: lua/config/pwsh.lua
     Problem: Assumes PowerShell 7+ and specific paths exist
     Impact: Silent failures on systems without pwsh
     Fix: Add existence checks:
       if vim.fn.executable('pwsh') == 0 then
         -- fallback logic
       end

[H3✅] LSP CONFIGURATION DUPLICATION
     Locations: lua/config/lsp.lua vs lua/plugins/lsp.lua
     Problem: config/lsp.lua is mostly commented but still loaded
     Impact: Confusion about which config is active
     Fix: Delete config/lsp.lua or make it explicitly commented as "OLD"

[H4✅] DEPRECATED FILES STILL IN CODEBASE
     Files:
       - lua/-settings/ (entire directory)
       - lua/config/keymaps.lua (use lua/keymaps/ instead)
       - lua/config/ui.lua (might be unused)
     Problem: Clutters codebase, confusing to maintain
     Impact: Developer confusion, potential conflicts
     Fix: Delete after confirming they're not sourced


MEDIUM PRIORITY
---------------
[M1] INCONSISTENT PATH SEPARATORS on Windows
     Problem: Mix of "/" and "\" throughout codebase
     Locations: Various lua files
     Impact: May cause path resolution issues on Windows
     Fix: Use vim.fn.expand() or vim.loop.os_uname() for platform detection

[M2] MASSIVE FILE SIZES - Hard to maintain
     Files:
       - lua/keymaps/core.lua (346 lines)
       - lua/plugins/telescope.lua (364 lines)
       - lua/plugins/lsp.lua (260 lines)
       - lua/plugins/cmp.lua (260 lines)
     Problem: Difficult to navigate and modify
     Impact: Slower maintenance, harder to debug
     Fix: Split into logical sub-files:
       - core.lua → core_editing.lua + core_windows.lua
       - telescope.lua → telescope_pickers.lua + telescope_keymaps.lua

[M3] COMMENTED CODE BLOAT
     Locations: Throughout config files, especially:
       - lua/config/lsp.lua (50+ lines of commented code)
       - lua/plugins/lsp.lua (commented alternatives)
     Problem: Makes code harder to read
     Impact: Confusion about what's active
     Fix: Move old code to git history, add brief comments explaining changes

[M4] SNIPPET FILE INCOMPLETE
     Location: lua/snippets/python.lua
     Problem: File exists but has minimal content
     Impact: Missing productivity tool for Python development
     Fix: Add common Python snippets (class, def, if __name__, docstrings)

[M5] WHICH-KEY NOT FULLY UTILIZED
     Problem: which-key installed but no group definitions in keymap files
     Impact: User doesn't see organized keymap hints
     Fix: Add which-key.register() calls with group names:
       which_key.register({
         ["<leader>s"] = { name = "+sessions" },
         ["<leader>f"] = { name = "+find" },
       })

[M6] NO SESSION VALIDATION
     Location: lua/plugins/session_manager_dev.lua
     Problem: Sessions may load with missing files/buffers
     Impact: Error messages on session restore
     Fix: Add pre_load hook to validate session contents


LOW PRIORITY
------------
[L1] MIXED LANGUAGE COMMENTS (Polish/English)
     Problem: Inconsistent documentation language
     Impact: Harder for international contributors
     Fix: Standardize on English for comments

[L2] MISSING DOCSTRINGS
     Problem: Custom functions lack documentation
     Locations: Custom functions in pwsh.lua, session manager
     Impact: Hard to understand intent
     Fix: Add LuaDoc style comments:
       --- Description
       ---@param name type
       ---@return type

[L3] NO CUSTOM FILETYPE CONFIGURATIONS
     Problem: No per-filetype indentation, formatting rules
     Impact: Inconsistent editing experience across languages
     Fix: Create ftplugin/ directory with language-specific configs

[L4] TELESCOPE PREVIEW NO LINE NUMBERS
     Problem: Preview window has no line numbers by default
     Impact: Harder to reference specific lines
     Fix: Add to telescope defaults:
       layout_config = {
         preview_cutoff = 1,
       }

[L5] NO KEYBINDING FOR COPILOT TOGGLE
     Problem: Copilot.vim installed but no toggle shortcut
     Impact: Can't easily enable/disable Copilot
     Fix: Add to keymaps:
       vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>ct', ':Copilot toggle<CR>')


================================================================================
3. IMPROVEMENTS
================================================================================

CODE QUALITY
------------
1. Add LuaLS type annotations for better LSP support
   Example:
     ---@type table<string, string>
     local formatters = {
       lua = 'stylua',
       python = 'black',
     }

2. Extract magic numbers/strings to constants
   Create lua/config/constants.lua:
     return {
       SEPARATOR_WIDTH = 72,
       SESSION_DIR = vim.fn.stdpath('data') .. '/persisted',
       DEFAULT_INDENT = 2,
     }

3. Create utility functions for repeated patterns
   Example: Path construction, keymap setting, autocmd creation

4. Add input validation for custom functions
   Example: Check if session name is valid before saving


PERFORMANCE
-----------
1. Lazy load more plugins
   - copilot.vim: Load only on InsertEnter or explicit command
   - undotree: Load only when :UndotreeToggle is called
   - diffview: Load on Git command

2. Disable unused treesitter features
   - If not using text objects, disable that module
   - If not using rainbow brackets, disable rainbow plugin

3. Use vim.schedule() for non-critical autocmds
   Example: Transparency setup can be scheduled

4. Consider switching from blink.cmp to mini.completion for even faster startup
   (Only if blink.cmp is too slow, which is unlikely)

5. Use telescope's preview_cutoff to disable preview for large result sets


MAINTAINABILITY
---------------
1. Create a lua/core/ directory for core utilities
   Structure:
     lua/core/
       ├── utils.lua (general utilities)
       ├── paths.lua (path utilities, platform detection)
       ├── keymaps.lua (keymap utilities)
       └── lsp.lua (LSP utilities)

2. Implement plugin configuration templates
   Create lua/templates/plugin_spec.lua:
     return {
       'user/plugin',
       event = 'VeryLazy',
       opts = {},
       config = function(_, opts)
         require('plugin').setup(opts)
       end,
     }

3. Add version pinning for critical plugins
   Example in lazy-lock.json (already exists, good!)
   Ensure you commit lazy-lock.json to git

4. Create make file or script for common tasks
   tasks.lua or Makefile:
     - Update plugins
     - Run health checks
     - Clean cache
     - Export keymap documentation

5. Add local configuration support
   Create lua/local.lua (gitignored):
     return {
       paths = {
         scripts = 'E:/Tools/BPY_Scripts/',
         code = 'C:/code_data/',
       },
       enable_copilot = true,
       enable_tmp = false,
     }


FEATURES
--------
1. Add project-local configuration support
   Check for .nvim.lua in project root and load it
   Example:
     local project_config = vim.fn.findfile('.nvim.lua', '.;')
     if project_config ~= '' then
       dofile(vim.fn.fnamemodify(project_config, ':p'))
     end

2. Implement custom command palette
   Create a telescope picker for all custom commands:
     - :Ss, :Sr, :Sl
     - Lazy commands
     - Mason commands
     - Custom functions

3. Add debug configuration
   Use nvim-dap for debugging:
     - Python debugging with debugpy
     - Node.js debugging
     - Lua debugging for plugin development

4. Create custom health check
   lua/health/nvimdev.lua:
     local M = {}
     function M.check()
       vim.health.start('nvim_dev configuration')
       -- Check for required executables
       -- Check for path conflicts
       -- Check for deprecated files
     end
     return M

5. Add buffer-local keymaps for specific filetypes
   Example: In Python files, add pytest shortcuts

6. Implement smart session naming
   Instead of directory-based, allow:
     - Project name from git remote
     - Custom session names
     - Multiple sessions per project

7. Add formatter selection picker
   Telescope picker to temporarily change formatter for current buffer


DOCUMENTATION
-------------
1. Create README.md in project root
   Sections:
     - Installation instructions
     - Prerequisites (Neovim version, external tools)
     - Directory structure explanation
     - Customization guide
     - Troubleshooting
     - Performance tips

2. Add KEYBINDINGS.md
   Auto-generate from keymap files using script

3. Create PLUGINS.md
   List all plugins with:
     - Purpose
     - Load trigger
     - Configuration location
     - Custom keybindings

4. Add inline documentation for complex functions
   Use LuaDoc format for LSP support

5. Create CONTRIBUTING.md
   Guidelines for:
     - Adding new plugins
     - Creating keymap files
     - Code style
     - Testing changes


ROBUSTNESS
----------
1. Add error handling wrappers
   Create lua/core/safe_require.lua:
     return function(module)
       local ok, result = pcall(require, module)
       if not ok then
         vim.notify('Failed to load ' .. module, vim.log.levels.ERROR)
         return nil
       end
       return result
     end

2. Validate external dependencies on startup
   Check for:
     - ripgrep (rg)
     - fzf
     - git
     - Node.js (for some LSPs)
     - Required LSP servers

3. Add fallback configurations
   If plugin fails to load, provide minimal functionality

4. Implement graceful degradation
   Example: If telescope fails, fall back to vim.ui.select()

5. Add logging for debugging
   Create lua/core/log.lua using vim.notify or plenary.log


TESTING
-------
1. Add unit tests for custom functions
   Use plenary.test_harness:
     tests/
       ├── utils_spec.lua
       ├── session_spec.lua
       └── keymaps_spec.lua

2. Create integration tests
   Test plugin interactions:
     - LSP + Completion
     - Telescope + Git
     - Sessions + Multiple buffers

3. Add performance benchmarks
   Measure:
     - Startup time (use --startuptime)
     - LSP response time
     - Telescope search speed

4. Implement visual regression tests
   Use screenshots for UI elements

5. Add CI/CD pipeline
   GitHub Actions to:
     - Run tests on each commit
     - Check for deprecated Neovim APIs
     - Validate plugin versions


================================================================================
4. RECOMMENDED TOOLS
================================================================================

ESSENTIAL (Install Immediately)
-------------------------------
1. lazy-check: Built-in lazy.nvim health check
   Usage: :Lazy check
   Purpose: Verify all plugins are correctly loaded

2. checkhealth: Neovim's built-in health checker
   Usage: :checkhealth
   Purpose: Verify LSP, treesitter, and other integrations

3. plenary.test: Testing framework (already installed)
   Usage: :PlenaryBustedDirectory tests/
   Purpose: Run unit tests for custom functions

4. nvim-treesitter-playground: Treesitter inspection
   Install: Add to lua/plugins/treesitter-playground.lua
   Purpose: Debug treesitter queries, inspect AST

5. vim-startuptime: Startup profiler
   Install: Add to lua/plugins/dev-tools.lua
   Usage: :StartupTime
   Purpose: Identify slow plugins and configs


DEVELOPMENT TOOLS
-----------------
1. nvim-dap: Debug Adapter Protocol
   Install: Add lua/plugins/dap.lua
   Purpose: Debug Python, JavaScript, and other languages
   Recommended UI: nvim-dap-ui

2. neodev.nvim or lazydev.nvim: Neovim Lua development
   Status: lazydev.nvim already installed ✓
   Purpose: Better Neovim API completions

3. luacheck: Lua static analysis
   Install: cargo install luacheck or Mason
   Usage: Run from command line or via null-ls
   Purpose: Catch errors in Lua configs

4. stylua: Lua formatter (already configured in conform.nvim ✓)
   Purpose: Consistent Lua code formatting

5. selene: Advanced Lua linter
   Install: cargo install selene or Mason
   Purpose: Better than luacheck, understands Neovim APIs


MAINTENANCE TOOLS
-----------------
1. plenary.reload: Hot reload Lua modules
   Usage: :lua require('plenary.reload').reload_module('module_name')
   Purpose: Faster config development (already using via F9)

2. mason-update-all: Update all Mason packages
   Create custom command in lua/config/lsp.lua:
     vim.api.nvim_create_user_command('MasonUpdateAll', function()
       vim.cmd('MasonUpdate')
     end, {})

3. lazy-profile: Plugin load time profiler
   Usage: :Lazy profile
   Purpose: Identify slow plugins

4. :messages: View error messages
   Create keymap: <leader>vm
   Purpose: Debug startup errors

5. nvim-notify: Better notifications (consider adding)
   Install: Add to lua/plugins/ui.lua
   Purpose: Prettier error/info messages with history


QUALITY ASSURANCE
-----------------
1. luassert: Assertion library for tests
   Status: Included with plenary
   Purpose: Write test assertions

2. mini.doc: Auto-generate documentation
   Install: Add mini.doc to lua/plugins/mini.lua
   Purpose: Generate docs from annotations

3. nvim-lint: Linting framework
   Install: Alternative to conform.nvim for diagnostics
   Purpose: Run linters on save

4. telescope-undo: Undo tree in telescope
   Install: Add to lua/plugins/telescope.lua
   Purpose: Better undo visualization than undotree

5. regexplainer.nvim: Explain regex patterns
   Install: Add to lua/plugins/dev-tools.lua
   Purpose: Understand complex regex in code


OPTIONAL (Nice to Have)
-----------------------
1. nvim-bqf: Better quickfix window
   Purpose: Enhanced quickfix with preview

2. trouble.nvim: Better diagnostics UI
   Purpose: Aggregate all diagnostics in one panel

3. todo-comments.nvim: Highlight TODO/FIXME/etc
   Purpose: Track technical debt

4. persistence.nvim: Alternative session manager
   Purpose: Compare with persisted.nvim if issues continue

5. harpoon: Quick file navigation
   Purpose: Mark and jump to frequent files

6. vim-matchup: Better % matching
   Purpose: Jump between function start/end, if/endif

7. nvim-surround: Better surround (alternative to current)
   Purpose: More powerful surround operations

8. flash.nvim: Better search/jump motions
   Purpose: Replace default search with fuzzy jump


WINDOWS-SPECIFIC
----------------
1. pwsh: PowerShell 7+
   Status: Already configured ✓
   Purpose: Modern shell for Windows

2. scoop: Windows package manager
   Install: https://scoop.sh
   Purpose: Easy installation of ripgrep, fzf, git, etc.
   Usage: scoop install ripgrep fzf git nodejs

3. gsudo: Sudo for Windows
   Install: scoop install gsudo
   Purpose: Run commands as admin from Neovim

4. wt (Windows Terminal): Better terminal
   Purpose: Better PowerShell/Neovim terminal experience

5. winget: Official Windows package manager
   Purpose: Alternative to scoop for tool installation


MONITORING & DEBUGGING
----------------------
1. :lua print(vim.inspect(obj)): Inspect Lua objects
   Create keymap helper for quick debugging

2. :verbose map <key>: Find keymap conflicts
   Create custom command to audit all keymaps

3. :lua vim.lsp.buf_notify(): Debug LSP communication
   Purpose: See what LSP server receives/sends

4. :lua vim.pretty_print(): Pretty print Lua values
   Purpose: Better than print() for complex objects

5. plenary.profile: Function profiler
   Usage: require('plenary.profile').start()
   Purpose: Profile custom function performance


================================================================================
5. FILE STRUCTURE / ORGANIZATION
================================================================================

CURRENT STRUCTURE ANALYSIS
--------------------------
✓ Good: Modular organization with clear separation
✓ Good: Plugin specs auto-discovered by lazy.nvim
✓ Good: Keymaps split into logical files
✗ Bad: Experimental code (tmp/) loaded in production
✗ Bad: Deprecated files (-settings/) still present
✗ Bad: Some files too large (346+ lines)


RECOMMENDED STRUCTURE
---------------------

nvim_dev/
├── init.lua                          # Main entry (keep minimal)
├── lazy-lock.json                    # Plugin versions (commit to git)
├── .gitignore                        # Ignore data/, .DS_Store, local.lua
├── README.md                         # Documentation (create)
├── KEYBINDINGS.md                    # Keymap reference (generate)
├── PLUGINS.md                        # Plugin list (generate)
│
├── lua/
│   ├── core/                         # NEW: Core utilities
│   │   ├── init.lua                  # Auto-load all core modules
│   │   ├── utils.lua                 # General utilities
│   │   ├── paths.lua                 # Path handling, platform detection
│   │   ├── keymaps.lua               # Keymap utilities
│   │   ├── lsp.lua                   # LSP utilities
│   │   ├── safe_require.lua          # Safe module loading
│   │   └── log.lua                   # Logging utilities
│   │
│   ├── config/                       # Configuration files
│   │   ├── settings.lua              # Vim options (keep)
│   │   ├── autocmd.lua               # Autocommands (expand)
│   │   ├── lazy.lua                  # Plugin manager setup (keep)
│   │   ├── lsp.lua                   # DELETE or mark as deprecated
│   │   ├── pwsh.lua                  # PowerShell config (refactor)
