CONTINUOUS TESTING
------------------
Set up automated testing in development workflow:

1. Pre-commit hook (create .git/hooks/pre-commit):
   ```bash
   #!/bin/bash
   echo "Running Neovim health checks..."
   nvim --headless -c "checkhealth nvimdev" -c "qa" || {
     echo "Health check failed! Commit aborted."
     exit 1
   }
   ```

2. Weekly plugin updates:
   - Schedule time to run :Lazy update
   - Run full test suite after update
   - Commit new lazy-lock.json if stable

3. Documentation updates:
   - After adding plugins, update PLUGINS.md
   - After changing keymaps, regenerate KEYBINDINGS.md
   - After refactoring, update README.md

4. Performance monitoring:
   - Monthly startup time check
   - Log and compare over time
   - Investigate if > 20% regression


SAFE TESTING ENVIRONMENT
-------------------------
Test risky changes in isolated environment:

Method 1: Separate config directory
  NVIM_APPNAME=nvim_test nvim
  This uses: ~/.config/nvim_test/ instead of ~/.config/nvim/

Method 2: Docker container
  docker run -it --rm -v $(pwd):/config alpine/neovim
  Test config in clean environment

Method 3: Virtual machine
  Use Windows Sandbox or Linux VM
  Test Windows-specific features safely

Method 4: Git branches
  git checkout -b test-new-feature
  Make changes, test thoroughly
  Merge to main only when stable


TESTING TOOLS SETUP
--------------------

Install testing dependencies:
  1. plenary.nvim (already installed)
  2. Add test runner to lua/plugins/dev_tools.lua:
     ```lua
     return {
       'nvim-lua/plenary.nvim',
       keys = {
         {
           '<leader>tt',
           '<cmd>PlenaryBustedDirectory tests/<cr>',
           desc = 'Run all tests'
         },
       },
     }
     ```

Create test helper utility:
  tests/helpers.lua:
    ```lua
    local M = {}

    function M.setup_test_env()
      -- Set up clean environment for tests
      vim.cmd('set noswapfile')
      vim.opt.runtimepath:append(vim.fn.stdpath('config'))
    end

    function M.cleanup_test_env()
      -- Clean up after tests
      vim.cmd('%bwipeout!')
    end

    return M
    ```


================================================================================
7. OTHER IMPORTANT NOTES
================================================================================

PLATFORM-SPECIFIC CONSIDERATIONS
--------------------------------

Windows/MSYS2 Environment:
  - Tree-sitter compilation requires MinGW64
  - Path separator handling crucial (/ vs \)
  - PowerShell encoding issues (UTF-8 vs code pages)
  - Line ending differences (CRLF vs LF)
  - Case-insensitive filesystem may cause issues
  - Symlinks require admin privileges
  - Performance generally slower than Linux

Recommendations:
  1. Ensure MSYS2 /mingw64/bin in PATH for tree-sitter
  2. Use vim.loop.os_uname() for platform detection
  3. Normalize paths with vim.fn.fnamemodify(path, ':p')
  4. Set 'fileformat' to 'unix' in settings.lua
  5. Use WSL2 for better performance if possible


NEOVIM 0.11+ CHANGES
--------------------
Your config uses modern Neovim 0.11+ APIs:

1. Native LSP API (vim.lsp.config, vim.lsp.enable)
   - Replaces lspconfig's setup() function
   - More efficient and built-in
   - Ensure you're on Neovim 0.11+ (check with :version)

2. vim.health instead of health module
   - Old: require('health')
   - New: vim.health
   - Add compatibility check:
     local health = vim.health or require('health')

3. Deprecation warnings to watch:
   - vim.lsp.buf.formatting() → vim.lsp.buf.format()
   - Some telescope APIs changed
   - Treesitter query syntax updates

Action: Run :checkhealth regularly to catch deprecated API usage


PLUGIN ECOSYSTEM CHANGES
-------------------------

Blink.cmp vs nvim-cmp:
  - You're using blink.cmp (newer, faster)
  - Less mature than nvim-cmp
  - May have compatibility issues with some sources
  - Watch GitHub issues: saghen/blink.cmp

Tree-sitter Main Branch:
  - You're using main branch (cutting edge)
  - May have breaking changes
  - Consider pinning to stable release:
    commit = '<stable_commit_hash>'

Mason Tool Installer:
  - Mason doesn't auto-install on first run
  - Consider adding mason-tool-installer.nvim:
    ```lua
    {
      'WhoIsSethDaniel/mason-tool-installer.nvim',
      opts = {
        ensure_installed = {
          'lua-language-server',
          'stylua',
          'pyright',
          'black',
          -- ... all your tools
        },
      },
    }
    ```


GIT WORKFLOW RECOMMENDATIONS
----------------------------

What to commit:
  ✓ lazy-lock.json (plugin versions)
  ✓ All .lua config files
  ✓ .gitignore
  ✓ README.md, documentation
  ✓ scripts/ directory
  ✓ tests/ directory

What NOT to commit:
  ✗ plugin/ directory (lazy.nvim installs here)
  ✗ data/ directory (sessions, undo files, etc.)
  ✗ .DS_Store, Thumbs.db
  ✗ local.lua (machine-specific config)
  ✗ tmp/ directory (experimental code)
  ✗ *.swp, *.swo (swap files)

Recommended .gitignore:
  ```
  # Plugin installation directory
  plugin/

  # Neovim data
  data/
  shada/

  # Local config
  lua/config/local.lua

  # Temporary files
  lua/tmp/
  *.swp
  *.swo
  *.swn
  *~

  # OS files
  .DS_Store
  Thumbs.db

  # Session files (if not using git-based sessions)
  sessions/

  # Logs
  *.log
  ```

Branch strategy:
  - main: Stable, tested configuration
  - dev: Testing new features
  - feature/*: Specific features/experiments
  - hotfix/*: Bug fixes

Commit messages:
  - Use conventional commits:
    feat(lsp): add rust-analyzer configuration
    fix(telescope): resolve keymap conflict
    refactor(keymaps): split core.lua into multiple files
    docs(readme): add installation instructions


BACKUP STRATEGY
----------------
Your Neovim config is code - treat it like source code:

1. Git repository (already have it) ✓
   - Push to GitHub/GitLab regularly
   - Add remote: git remote add origin <url>

2. External backups:
   - Cloud storage (Google Drive, Dropbox)
   - External drive
   - Different machine

3. Session data:
   - Sessions in data/persisted/ are not in git
   - Consider backing up important sessions separately
   - Or use git-based session manager

4. Plugin cache:
   - lazy.nvim stores plugins in data/lazy/
   - Don't need to backup (can reinstall)
   - But keep lazy-lock.json in git

5. Mason tools:
   - Mason stores in data/mason/
   - Don't backup (reinstall with :Mason)
   - But document required tools

Recovery process:
  1. Clone git repo: git clone <url> ~/.config/nvim
  2. Open Neovim (lazy.nvim auto-installs)
  3. Run :Lazy restore (uses lazy-lock.json)
  4. Run :Mason (install language servers)
  5. Copy local.lua from backup


SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
-----------------------

Sensitive data:
  - Never commit API keys, tokens, passwords
  - Use environment variables:
    local api_key = os.getenv('OPENAI_API_KEY')
  - Store in local.lua (gitignored)

Plugin sources:
  - Only install plugins from trusted sources
  - Review plugin code before installing
  - Check GitHub stars, last update, issues
  - Be cautious with plugins that run shell commands

Mason tools:
  - Language servers run with your privileges
  - Can access your files
  - Install from official Mason registry only

Remote code execution:
  - Some plugins (copilot, ChatGPT) send code to servers
  - Be aware of data leaving your machine
  - Use company-approved tools only for work projects

Recommendations:
  1. Review plugins in lua/plugins/ for unknown code
  2. Use Neovim's sandbox for untrusted configs
  3. Keep Neovim and plugins updated for security patches
  4. Don't run Neovim as root/admin


DEBUGGING TECHNIQUES
--------------------

When something breaks:

1. Check messages:
   :messages
   Look for errors, warnings

2. Enable debug logging:
   vim.lsp.set_log_level('debug')
   Check LSP log: :lua vim.cmd('e ' .. vim.lsp.get_log_path())

3. Disable plugins:
   Temporarily disable suspect plugin in lua/plugins/
   Add: enabled = false to plugin spec

4. Bisect plugins:
   Comment out half of plugins
   Restart, test
   Narrow down culprit

5. Profile startup:
   nvim --startuptime /tmp/startup.log
   Find slow plugins

6. Check plugin health:
   :checkhealth <plugin>
   E.g., :checkhealth telescope

7. Reload config:
   F9 key (your custom reload function)
   Or restart Neovim

8. Use minimal config:
   Create minimal_init.lua with only problem plugin
   nvim -u minimal_init.lua

9. Check plugin updates:
   :Lazy show <plugin>
   Look at recent commits for breaking changes

10. Search issues:
    Check plugin GitHub issues
    Search for error message


PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION TIPS
------------------------------

Current performance is likely good, but to optimize further:

1. Lazy load everything possible:
   - Use 'event', 'cmd', 'ft', 'keys' triggers
   - Avoid 'VeryLazy' if more specific trigger exists

2. Disable unused features:
   - Tree-sitter modules you don't use
   - LSP servers you don't need
   - Completion sources you don't use

3. Reduce scope:
   - Limit telescope search scope with cwd
   - Disable treesitter for large files (>5000 lines)
   - Use simple colorscheme for better performance

4. Profile and optimize:
   - :Lazy profile shows load times
   - Optimize slowest plugins first
   - Consider alternatives for slow plugins

5. Use faster alternatives:
   - fzf-native for telescope (already have) ✓
   - blink.cmp instead of nvim-cmp (already have) ✓
   - oil.nvim instead of nvim-tree (already have) ✓

6. Reduce syntax highlighting:
   - Disable rainbow brackets if not needed
   - Reduce treesitter highlights in large files

7. Optimize autocmds:
   - Use specific events, not *
   - Use vim.schedule for non-critical autocmds
   - Group related autocmds

8. Clean up regularly:
   - Remove unused plugins
   - Clear plugin cache: :Lazy clean
   - Remove old sessions


COLLABORATION TIPS
------------------

If sharing config with others or using on multiple machines:

1. Document customizations:
   - Add comments for non-obvious choices
   - Explain why specific plugins were chosen
   - Document keymap rationale

2. Provide local.example.lua:
   ```lua
   -- Copy this file to local.lua and customize
   return {
     paths = {
       scripts = 'path/to/scripts',
       code = 'path/to/code',
     },
     enable_copilot = true,
     enable_tmp = false,
   }
   ```

3. Make platform detection automatic:
   Don't hardcode Windows-only code
   Use: if vim.loop.os_uname().sysname == 'Windows_NT' then

4. Provide installation script:
   install.sh or install.ps1
   Installs dependencies, sets up config

5. Add troubleshooting guide:
   Common issues and solutions
   How to get help

6. Use consistent style:
   - 2-space indentation (Lua standard)
   - Single quotes for strings
   - Descriptive variable names


FUTURE-PROOFING
---------------

Prepare for Neovim and plugin evolution:

1. Pin critical plugins:
   For plugins you depend on, use commit hashes:
   ```lua
   {
     'user/plugin',
     commit = 'abc123', -- Known working version
   }
   ```

2. Monitor deprecations:
   - Run :checkhealth regularly
   - Read Neovim release notes
   - Follow plugin changelogs

3. Test before updating:
   - Read plugin CHANGELOG before :Lazy update
   - Update plugins one at a time
   - Test after each update

4. Document workarounds:
   If you have to work around a bug:
   -- WORKAROUND for issue #123 in plugin X
   -- Remove this when fixed
   <workaround code>

5. Stay modular:
   - Easy to swap plugins (interface-based design)
   - Don't tightly couple config to specific plugins

6. Follow best practices:
   - Use modern APIs (vim.keymap, vim.opt, etc.)
   - Avoid deprecated functions
   - Write Lua, not Vimscript


